    "evidence_text": "Introduction and related work\tLanguage models can be optimized to recognize syntax and semantics with great accuracy .\n\nWe use three creative English datasets with distinct linguistic characteristics: (1) A corpus of 740 classical and contemporary English poems,\n\nWe reserve 10% of our data for test set and another 10% for our validation set.\n\nlog-likelihood probabilities, as sampling has shown to produce better output quality .\n\nPlease refer to Supplementary Section Table TABREF6 for training parameters of each dataset and Table TABREF5 for hyperparameters of each encoder.\n\nWe pick these values after experimentation with our validation set.\n\nTraining and output generation code can be found online.\n\nEvaluation and conclusion\tEvaluating creative generation tasks is both critical and complex .\n\nAlong the lines of previous research on evaluating text generation tasks , we report the perplexity scores of our test set on the evaluated models in the Supplementary Section, Table TABREF4 Our model shows improvements over baseline and GumbelGAN.\n\nCommon computational methods like BLEU  and perplexity are at best a heuristic and not strong indicators of good performance in text generation models .\n\nParticularly, since these scores use target sequences as a reference, it has the same pitfalls as relying on MLE.",
